<% survey = @show.open_endedness_survey %>

<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  <%= link_to "On the substrate the programme already had", finding_path("replicator-complexity-plateau") %>,
  the dominant replicator's complexity is counted after emergence and the count is of
  directions: how many emerged worlds end more complicated than they started. That is
  the baseline. The open question it leaves is whether the substrate was what was limiting —
  whether a world that has to pay for computation, a world whose regions differ, or a world
  whose tapes are free to lengthen goes somewhere the default substrate never went. This
  page is that verdict, for the three sweeps DESIGN §1.3 built to ask it: sweep 6
  (instruction cost), sweep 7 (environmental structure) and sweep 8 (room to grow).
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  Each of the three sweeps carries its own control. The first arm of each grid is the
  substrate every earlier sweep ran — <span class="mono">energy_per_epoch</span> 0, a
  <span class="mono">uniform</span> world, <span class="mono">max_tape_len</span> equal to
  the tape length — so a treated arm is read against the default substrate inside the same
  experiment, at the same mutation rate, world size and step budget, rather than against
  runs from another sweep.
</p>
<p>
  For every <strong>emerged</strong> run of those sweeps this reads two series off the
  stored samples at or after that run's own emergence epoch. Emerged is narrower than
  flagged: the detector reads <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> alone, so a crossing
  counts here only when the replicator census or the copy rate backs it within ten samples
  of it (<span class="mono">docs/design_record.md</span>, 2026-09-15) — the flagged runs of
  the 512 arm whose census never left zero are not worlds that emerged. The two series
  are: <span class="mono">dominant_instruction_count</span>,
  how many bytes of the most populous tape that passes the replicator test the run's own
  instruction set executes, and
  <span class="mono">distinct_lineages</span>, how many lineage ids the cells hold. A run's
  <strong>peak</strong> is the highest reading its samples record after the crossing — a
  ceiling the run touched, not a level it settled at and not a fitted trend — and a run is
  <strong>still rising</strong> when its last reading is above the median of its own
  post-crossing readings. An arm is then placed by counting: a treated arm raises the peak
  when more than half of its measured runs read strictly above the median peak of the
  control arm's runs. Nothing on this page is fitted, and nothing is averaged across sweeps.
</p>
<p>
  A hypothesis reads <strong>supported</strong> as soon as one arm with at least
  <%= pluralize Findings::OpenEndednessSurvey::MIN_ARM_RUNS, "measured run" %> raises the
  peak. It reads <strong>not supported</strong> only when the control arm carries at least
  that many measured runs, no treated arm raises the peak, and every treated arm has been
  read — the refutation DESIGN states, with nothing left unseeded. Anything between the two
  reads <strong>unresolved</strong>, and the arms that were untestable are named.
</p>
<p>
  An arm is read either by being measured or by coming up empty. An arm whose runs all
  reached their last epoch —
  <%= pluralize Findings::OpenEndednessSurvey::MIN_BARREN_RUNS, "terminal run" %> or more,
  a whole seed-block of the grid — with no crossing confirmed in any of them
  <strong>never emerged</strong>, and such an arm counts against the hypothesis rather than
  leaving it untested: it holds no replicator whose complexity could stand above the control
  arm's, so it cannot be a substrate that raised the plateau. An arm with one emerged run,
  or with fewer terminal runs than that, is still untestable — one seed decides nothing in
  either direction. If the <em>control</em> arm never emerged the sweep reads unresolved
  whatever the other arms did, because there is no default substrate left to read them
  against.
</p>
<p>
  Each sweep is decided on the one observable its DESIGN hypothesis names. The other
  observable is printed underneath it in the same shape and carries no verdict, so both
  readings of every sweep are on the page without a second hypothesis being invented for
  them.
</p>

<p>
  Under each hypothesis the page also prints <strong>how often life emerged</strong> in each
  arm — the arm's confirmed crossings over the runs of that arm that reached an end — with a
  two-sided Fisher exact test of the arm's emerged and unemerged runs against the control
  arm's. That table is descriptive. It decides nothing about the plateau, which is read from
  the peaks of the runs that did emerge and not from how many of them there were, and no
  verdict on this page moves with it.
</p>

<h2>Why complexity is read in instructions and not in bytes</h2>
<p>
  The obvious reading — <span class="mono">dominant_compressed_len</span>, the dominant tape
  under zlib — is unusable on the sweeps this page decides. zlib wraps an incompressible
  stream in an 11-byte envelope, so a tape of junk compresses to its own length plus 11, and
  on every arm of the room-to-grow sweep that is exactly what 92–99.8% of post-crossing
  samples read: 75, 139, 267 and 523 bytes at caps of 64, 128, 256 and 512. The reading is
  the cap, not the replicator, and "523 bytes" cannot be told apart from 512 random bytes.
</p>
<p>
  <span class="mono">dominant_instruction_count</span> is not capped that way. Post-crossing
  it reads 9–17 ops at cap 64, 20–26 at 128, 17–28 at 256 and 37–52 at 512 — 14–26% of the
  tape at the smallest cap and 7–10% at the largest. Quadrupling the byte budget does not
  quadruple the op content; the added bytes are junk. So every verdict below is read off the
  instruction count, which is the pre-registered reading for "complexity keeps rising"
  (<span class="mono">docs/design_record.md</span>, 2026-09-16), and the compressed length is
  kept only as a length. Each run page now prints compressed length over the tape's own raw
  length beside it, so the saturation is visible rather than inferred.
</p>

<h2>Hypothesis 1 — instruction cost</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 6: <em>a cost pressure selects for efficient copiers and opens a second
  niche.</em> A second niche is a second way of making a living, and what this substrate
  records of that is the lineages it keeps alive, so the priced arms are read against the
  costless one on <span class="mono">distinct_lineages</span>.
</p>
<%= render "findings/substrate_verdict", bet: survey.instruction_cost,
           reading: "a lineage count", unit: "lineages" %>
<%= render "findings/substrate_reading", bet: survey.instruction_cost_complexity,
           reading: "complexity in instructions", unit: "instructions", decided_on: "lineages" %>
<%= render "findings/substrate_emergence", bet: survey.instruction_cost %>

<h2>Hypothesis 2 — environmental structure</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 7: <em>environmental structure raises the plateau the dominant
  replicator's complexity settles at, refuted if a world whose regions differ plateaus where
  a uniform world does.</em>
</p>
<%= render "findings/substrate_verdict", bet: survey.environmental_structure,
           reading: "a complexity in instructions", unit: "instructions" %>
<p>
  Its secondary prediction — <em>a heterogeneous world keeps more lineages alive after
  emergence</em> — is the same arms read on <span class="mono">distinct_lineages</span>, and
  DESIGN states it as a prediction, so this one does carry a verdict.
</p>
<%= render "findings/substrate_verdict", bet: survey.environmental_structure_lineages,
           reading: "a lineage count", unit: "lineages" %>
<%= render "findings/substrate_emergence", bet: survey.environmental_structure %>

<h2>Hypothesis 3 — room to grow</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 8: <em>room to grow raises the plateau the dominant replicator's
  complexity settles at, refuted if tapes free to lengthen plateau where fixed-length tapes
  do</em> — which would say the 64-byte ceiling was never the binding constraint.
</p>
<%= render "findings/substrate_verdict", bet: survey.room_to_grow,
           reading: "a complexity in instructions", unit: "instructions" %>
<%= render "findings/substrate_reading", bet: survey.room_to_grow_lineages,
           reading: "lineage count", unit: "lineages", decided_on: "complexity" %>
<%= render "findings/substrate_emergence", bet: survey.room_to_grow %>

<%# The 2026-09-19 corpus rescore, read from the stored samples of the sweep's 240 terminal
    runs (GitHub #174). %>
<% detector_rules = [
     { cap: 64, terminal: 30, constant: 3, relative: 3, replicators: 2 },
     { cap: 128, terminal: 90, constant: 5, relative: 5, replicators: 3 },
     { cap: 256, terminal: 90, constant: 4, relative: 4, replicators: 4 },
     { cap: 512, terminal: 30, constant: 30, relative: 1, replicators: 1 }
   ] %>

<h3>The same sweep under both detector rules</h3>
<p>
  This sweep was flagged by the detector's original rule, a constant threshold:
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> at or below 0.6, held. A fresh soup's ratio
  depends on the cap, and the dependence straddles that line — over its first 500 epochs the
  512 arm means 0.754 with a minimum of 0.618, against 0.984 at cap 64
  (<span class="mono">docs/design_record.md</span>, 2026-09-19). So a second reading was
  taken against each run's own baseline, the mean <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span>
  of its samples at epoch 500 or earlier, and the stored corpus was rescored under it
  without re-running anything. On 2026-09-21 that relative rule became the rule
  <span class="mono">transition_epoch</span> is read by, and the constant one the companion
  <span class="mono">transition_epoch_constant</span>. Both readings of this sweep:
</p>

<div class="table-scroll" id="max-tape-len-detector-rules" tabindex="0" role="region"
     aria-label="Crossings per max_tape_len arm under both detector rules">
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th><span class="mono">max_tape_len</span></th>
        <th class="numeric">Terminal runs</th>
        <th class="numeric">Crossings, companion constant rule</th>
        <th class="numeric">Crossings, transition rule</th>
        <th class="numeric">Confirmed by a replicator</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% detector_rules.each do |arm| %>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono"><%= arm.fetch(:cap) %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:terminal) %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:constant) %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:relative) %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:replicators) %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-muted">
  Rescored with <span class="mono">lab:backfill_transitions</span> over 1 729
  terminal runs of the whole corpus, 2026-09-19 (GitHub #174).
</p>

<p>
  The 512 arm's 30 constant crossings are the initial condition rather than anything that
  happened in the world: the arm starts within a hair of the constant threshold, and every
  one of those 30 runs crosses it by epoch 1 000. Under the rule the observable now reads
  that arm keeps <strong>1</strong> crossing — the same run the replicator census confirms. The three
  narrower caps read the same crossings under both rules, and so does every arm of the other
  six experiments, 35 arms in all. Over the whole rescored corpus — 1 729 terminal runs — the
  512 arm is the only place the two rules part, and there is no crossing the relative rule
  finds and the constant one misses.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>No verdict above moves.</strong> Emergence on this page has required a witness
  since the record's 2026-09-15 entries — the replicator census or the copy rate, read
  against every crossing a run holds — so the 512 arm's flagged-but-dead runs were never
  emerged runs here, and the arm has entered these tables as 1 of 30 all along. The peaks
  the three hypotheses are decided on are read from emerged runs only, and they are
  unchanged.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>What the flag counts cannot be read for.</strong> Under the transition rule the
  crossings by cap read 3 / 5 / 4 / 1 — flat, then falling at the widest cap — so nothing
  here says that a bigger cap transitions more often or sooner. "Room to grow" is read on
  this page for the height of the plateau the dominant replicator reaches, which is the
  verdict above, and not for how often life started.
  <span class="mono">transition_epoch</span> is the relative reading since the
  2026-09-21 record entry relocked it; the constant column beside it is the reading every
  finding was stated in before that.
</p>

<h2>Does anything keep rising?</h2>
<% if survey.any? %>
  <p>
    Across the three sweeps the lab holds
    <%= pluralize survey.flagged_count, "run" %> flagged by the detector and
    <%= survey.emerged_count %> confirmed by a replicator.
    <%= survey.measured_count(:instructions) %> of the confirmed ones carry a complexity reading
    after their crossing and <%= survey.rising_count(:instructions) %> end above the middle of their own
    post-crossing readings; <%= survey.measured_count(:lineages) %> carry a lineage count and
    <%= survey.rising_count(:lineages) %> end above the middle of theirs. That is a
    description of where each run ended, not a trend, and it is censored at both ends by that
    run's budget.
  </p>
<% else %>
  <p class="text-muted">
    No run of the three substrate sweeps has emerged in this database yet — a crossing the
    census or the copy rate confirms — so all three hypotheses are unresolved and nothing
    above is a result. The sweeps are queued; this page
    reads them at render time and fills in as their runs cross.
  </p>
<% end %>

<% if survey.rates_comparable? %>
  <p>
    Read as one table rather than three, the three control arms emerged in
    <%= survey.control_emerged_count %> of their
    <%= pluralize survey.control_terminal_count, "terminal run" %> and every treated arm
    together in <%= survey.treated_emerged_count %> of
    <%= pluralize survey.treated_terminal_count, "terminal run" %> — the default substrate
    against every substrate the programme bet on, pooled across sweeps that were never
    designed to be pooled, so it is a summary of this page and not a test.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2>What this does not claim</h2>
<p>
  <strong>A peak is a peak at these budgets.</strong> Every arm runs the same 20 000
  epochs as the sweeps it is compared to, and a run's series is cut off wherever its budget
  ended. A substrate that would have elaborated at epoch 200 000 reads flat here, and the
  refutation condition DESIGN states is met at this budget rather than in general.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>"Still rising" is a description, not a verdict.</strong> No hypothesis on this page
  turns on it. A run counts as rising when its last post-crossing reading is above the median
  of its post-crossing readings, which rules out a single step up right after the crossing
  but is still one reading against a summary of a short series rather than a trend, and it is
  censored at both ends: a world still climbing when its epochs ran out reads as rising, and
  one that would have climbed later reads as flat.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>A lower rate at 20 000 epochs is not an impossibility.</strong> How often life
  emerged is counted at the budget these sweeps ran, and an arm that emerged less often than
  its control may be slower rather than barren. The
  <%= link_to "60 000-epoch re-run of sweep 1", finding_path("mutation-rate-long-horizon") %>
  found half of that sweep's confirmed crossings after epoch 20 000, where the short sweep
  had already stopped watching — so every rate above is a rate of emergence by epoch 20 000,
  and an arm at 0 of 10 here is an arm nothing crossed in that window.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Counts, not effect sizes.</strong> A majority of an arm's runs above the control
  arm's median peak is a direction, not a magnitude, and at the seed counts these sweeps
  carry a direction is compatible with a large range of true effects. None of the three
  verdicts is a significance test.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Complexity is a count of instructions and lineages are descent.</strong> An
  instruction count bounds the program rather than describing it — a tape can carry fifty
  ops that do nothing useful — and
  <span class="mono">distinct_lineages</span> counts descent rather than shape — two
  lineages that drifted onto the same tape still count twice. Neither reading opens a tape
  and says what it does, so "a second niche" is inferred from a count of lineages rather
  than observed.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Every run is read from its own crossing.</strong> An arm that takes longer to
  cross is read from a later epoch, and <span class="mono">distinct_lineages</span> falls as
  one lineage takes over the world, so a substrate that delays emergence enters this table
  with fewer lineages left through no property of the niches it opened. Nothing here
  corrects for that, and hypothesis 1 is the one it bears on.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>One reading per sweep decides it.</strong> Each hypothesis is judged on the
  observable it names and nothing else; the second reading of each sweep is printed beside
  it and decides nothing. An arm that raises the complexity peak while losing lineages would
  read supported on sweep 7's primary prediction and not supported on its secondary one,
  which is two sentences about the same world rather than a contradiction.
</p>

<h2>What next</h2>
<p>
  If every arm reads where its control does, the three substrate bets are spent and the
  design record's rung 4 needs a richer substrate rather than a wider grid — and this page is
  the evidence for saying so. If an arm does raise the peak, the next run is that arm
  alone at a budget long enough to see whether the new level holds. Either way the reading
  that would settle it is per lineage rather than per dominant tape, so one colony can be
  watched elaborating instead of a population being scored by whichever tape is most
  populous at each sample.
</p>
